    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d84">

                <pb facs="0203" n="163" sameAs="frus:frus1865p1/pg163"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1265.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>. <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-02-21"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 21, 1865</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>. Your despatch of the 26th of January, No.
                    865, has been received. I thank you for the very careful survey it presents of
                    British national sentiment <pb facs="0204" n="164" xml:id="pg164"/> in regard to
                    our civil war, and for your judicious suggestions touching the con-duct of our
                    affairs in the present crisis. Judging from recent indications, the desire for
                    separation in the south is not now intense enough to induce a resort to the
                    measure of universal emancipation.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams</hi>. Esq.,<hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
